2,Opucikwar - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opucikwar,"The Pucikwar language, O-Puchikwar, is an extinct language of the Andaman Islands, India, formerly spoken by the Pucikwar people on the south coast of Middle Andaman, the northeast coast of South Andaman, and on Baratang Island. It belonged to the Great Andamanese family.",A-Pucikwar,Andamanese
3,Music of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_the_Andaman_and_Nicobar_Islands,"The music of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands are a mixture of the indigenous cultures of the islands, as well as more recent cultural influencers from the descendants of the early settlers in the island from the Indian subcontinent. Folk traditions of the area include that of the Moken seafarers and various kinds of ritual tribal dance.",A-Pucikwar,Andamanese
4,Traditional Music of Andaman and Nicobar Islands - Thrillophilia,https://www.thrillophilia.com/blog/andaman-nicobar-traditional-music/,"The traditional music of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands is a captivating blend of indigenous tribal rhythms and various multicultural influences. It highlights the diversity of music in songs, including Great Andamanese chants and Nicobari festival songs, providing examples of devotional and increasingly contemporary fusions, in presenting diversity as a point of celebration. The Duggera drum ...",A-Pucikwar,Andamanese
